Design Tools: Figma or Adobe XD for UI/UX mockups


Collaboration Tools: GitHub, Trello, Google Workspace


Testing Tools: Expo Go for mobile, Java Swing IDE for desktop simulation


HAVEN: UI/UX Design Overview
Design Philosophy
HAVEN’s UI/UX design centers around clarity, urgency, and empathy. Since it deals with emergencies involving pets, the interface must minimize cognitive load, communicate clearly under stress, and provide immediate access to help.
The overall design aesthetic follows a modern minimalist emergency interface style — dark mode for the desktop (for responders working in control rooms), and bright, intuitive red-white contrast for the mobile app (for pet owners).

MOBILE APP (Pet Owners)
Design Inspiration
Visual and interaction design is inspired by Callert and RESQ mobile UI samples.


Prioritizes accessibility, large touch targets, and intuitive flow.


Uses a clean red-and-white color palette with soft gradients and rounded components to establish a sense of urgency yet trust.


Core Pages
1. Home Screen / Emergency Button Interface
Dominant central red circular button labeled “EMERGENCY” or “PET IN DISTRESS.”


Minimal distractions, only essential options visible.


Subtext: “Press and hold to send alert” to prevent accidental triggers.


Subtle animated pulse around the button to create a sense of readiness.


Upon press, an animation plays (expanding ripple or glow), confirming alert dispatch.


Layout Structure:
Top: User profile thumbnail and name (quick access to account).


Center: Large emergency button.


Bottom: Navigation bar with icons for Home, Report, Map, Profile.



2. Emergency Form Screen
For non-critical or secondary reports (e.g., “Lost pet,” “Minor injury,” “Animal stranded”).


Multi-step structured form:


Category selection (injury, lost pet, rescue, etc.)


Description box


Upload photo/video option


Pet details (species, color, breed, etc.)


Optional location correction (using Google Maps pin)


Clean card-based form layout for visual grouping.



3. Live Map Screen
Displays real-time incident markers (pulsing icons) representing nearby pet-related emergencies.


Users can tap a marker to view summary info (type, status, responders).


Uses Mapbox or Google Maps API for smooth navigation and zoom.


Color-coded alerts:


Red: Critical emergency


Yellow: Ongoing response


Green: Resolved


A toggle allows users to switch between “List View” and “Map View.”



4. Profile and History Screen
Shows user’s registered contact details and pet profiles.


Displays a history of previous reports (timestamp, type, and resolution).


Option to update details or delete reports.



UX Interaction Highlights
One-click emergency flow: Button → Confirmation → Alert sent → Notification feedback.


Location auto-detection: Fetches GPS instantly after pressing emergency.


Smooth transitions: Fade and slide animations between screens for clarity.


Accessibility: High-contrast mode, haptic feedback, and voice accessibility for disabled users.



DESKTOP APP (Emergency Response Unit)
Design Inspiration
Based on the dark-mode mapping dashboards shown in the first two images.


Interface is professional, data-oriented, and optimized for widescreen monitoring setups.


Color palette: charcoal black, muted blue, and emergency red highlights.



Core Interface Layout
1. Live Mapping Dashboard (Primary Screen)
Central focus: a real-time interactive map showing emergency alerts within the area of responsibility.


Each alert appears as a pulsing red dot (critical) or yellow dot (minor).


Hovering or clicking displays a tooltip with details (report ID, user, pet type, location, time).


Right-hand panel lists active cases chronologically, with status indicators (NEW, IN RESPONSE, RESOLVED).


Filter options for “Critical Only,” “Ongoing,” “Resolved,” and “All Reports.”


Zoom and pan enabled for precise location tracking.


Map Visualization Elements:
Animated response vehicle icons (ambulance, rescue, vet van) with real-time tracking.


Area outlines highlighting coverage zones per unit.


Optional weather or traffic overlay for operational awareness.



2. Incident Log Page
Displays complete records of all alerts received, filterable by date, location, or type.


Each record links to detailed view including:


User info (name, contact, address)


Pet info


Incident summary and timestamp


Actions taken and notes from responders


Export to CSV or PDF for documentation.



3. Statistics Dashboard
Provides visual analytics of response performance and incident distribution.


Components:


Total alerts this month


Average response time


Breakdown by incident type (injury, rescue, lost pet, etc.)


Heatmap visualization overlay on the map


Chart libraries: Recharts or Chart.js for responsive visuals.



4. User Management Page
Database of all registered HAVEN users.


Searchable by name, contact, or location.


Allows editing of verified responders, assigned zones, and permissions.



5. Communication and Coordination Interface
In-dashboard messaging system to directly reach out to the pet owner who sent the report.


Simple chat interface on right panel, enabling quick response and photo sharing.


“Call Now” button integrated using VoIP API or tel: protocol for immediate contact.



UX Principles
Cognitive Clarity: Every alert and icon communicates a single clear message.


Visual Hierarchy: Red pulsing markers grab attention, followed by detailed lists on the right.


Consistency: Unified dark theme, sans-serif typography (Inter or Roboto), and icon-based navigation.


Responsiveness: Scales to both single-monitor and multi-screen control centers.



